\begin{abstract}
In flexible job shop scheduling that captures processing order with an operation-precedence graph (operation order within each job and processing order on each machine)
and records the intervals in which each vehicle occupies each corridor in a time-window reservation table,
the standard way to delineate the rescheduling scope after a disturbance is still affected-operation rescheduling (AOR):
it first identifies the operations that can no longer be executed as planned because of the disturbance, and then adds their successors along the operation-precedence graph.
Disturbances such as corridor blockage or corridor slowdown act only on corridors; machines and operations remain available,
so AOR finds no affected operation, returns an empty rescheduling scope, and concludes that no rescheduling is needed.
In fact, the planned corridor occupancies that overlap the disturbed interval are already invalid and the original schedule is infeasible.
Moreover, since a corridor admits only one vehicle at a time, a vehicle planned to traverse it later must wait for the preceding vehicle to leave (hereafter, a yielding relation);
once the preceding vehicle is delayed, the following one is delayed in turn, and the delay passes from vehicle to vehicle along yielding relations.
The problem is not that the disturbance goes undetected (the blockage itself is known), but that the operation-precedence graph on which AOR delineates the scope contains only operations and their precedence relations;
it has neither corridor occupancies nor the yielding relations among them, and therefore cannot express this kind of invalidation or its propagation.

We instead delineate the rescheduling scope at the level of corridor occupancies and propose the spatiotemporal reservation closure (\STRC); the set of occupancies it outputs is called the reservation impact set.
It is constructed as follows: starting from the planned corridor occupancies whose occupancy intervals overlap the disturbance window, it follows four kinds of dependency relations among occupancies---yielding on the same corridor,
the next occupancy of the same vehicle, the occupancy of the next operation of the same job, and the occupancy of the next operation on the same machine---and keeps adding successor occupancies
until no new occupancy can be added (i.e., it takes the transitive closure).
In the original plan, each corridor occupancy is determined by three items: the corridor traversed (i.e., the path), the occupancy interval, and the carrying vehicle. Occupancies inside the impact set may have their path and interval replanned
(and, under disturbances such as vehicle failure, their vehicle replaced); occupancies outside it keep all three items of the original plan.
Once the four kinds of dependency relations are fixed, whether an occupancy belongs to the reservation impact set depends only on whether it is reachable from the seeds along these relations; the result is unique
and does not depend on which rerouting algorithm is used afterwards.
Bounded repair on the impact set has two levels: it first keeps the original vehicle assignment and replans conflict-free paths only for the occupancies inside the set; if the schedule is still infeasible,
it successively adds the subsequent occupancies of the same job, the subsequent occupancies of the same vehicle, and finally all occupancies unfinished at the decision time to the rewritable scope, retrying at each step.

Experiments use \NSeeds\ random seeds on each of \NInst\ instances (the random seed determines disturbance injection and the search process), giving \NPairs\ instance--seed pairs.
Under corridor blockage, the rescheduling scope delineated by AOR on the operation-precedence graph is empty on all \NPairs\ pairs, although the original schedule is in fact infeasible on every one of them.
With the rerouting procedure unchanged and only the scope object switched from the operation-precedence graph to the reservation impact set, the number of pairs on which repair yields a feasible schedule rises from \FeasRone\ to \FeasRtwo.
On all \NPairs\ pairs the reservation impact set satisfies two properties: first, it is closed, i.e., no dependency edge leads from an occupancy inside the set to one outside it;
second, after repair, the corridor, interval, and vehicle of every occupancy outside the set coincide with the original plan.
If, with everything else unchanged, the rewritable set is replaced by ``all occupancies unfinished at the decision time'', runtime and makespan remain almost unchanged,
but the fraction of rewritten occupancies rises from \ResFracCheapRtwo\ to \ResFracAllFuture: among the \NPairs\ pairs, scoping by the reservation impact set rewrites fewer, equally many, or more occupancies on
\RAStabWTL\ pairs, respectively (two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, \(p=\RAStabP\)).
We also compare with warm-started global rescheduling, which takes the original schedule as the initial solution, re-searches machine assignment and processing order within a given time limit, and likewise does not rewrite finished occupancies.
Bounded repair is two to three orders of magnitude faster, but its makespan \(C_{\max}\) (the completion time of all jobs) is never better than that of global rescheduling under any tested time limit (\(0.2\)--\(2\)\,s);
since global rescheduling already wins at the shortest time limit and its \(C_{\max}\) can only decrease as the limit is relaxed, bounded repair cannot overtake it under longer limits either.

Hence, the restoration of feasibility comes from replacing the scope object: with the rerouting procedure unchanged, switching the scope object from the operation-precedence graph to the reservation impact set restores feasibility.
The reduction in rewrites comes from the reservation impact set being smaller than ``all unfinished occupancies''; in our measurements, however, the former already covers about \ClosureAliveFrac\ of the latter on average,
excluding less than one tenth of the occupancies, so the reduction is limited, and on instances where the impact set approaches all unfinished occupancies the two nearly coincide and the reduction disappears.
Rerouting inside the set, keeping the original plan outside it, and enlarging the rewritable scope after a failed repair all follow existing local rescheduling practice;
the contribution of this paper lies in re-determining which kind of object the rescheduling scope should be delineated on, namely replacing the set of operations with the reservation impact set formed by corridor occupancies.
\end{abstract}

