\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
%% =====================================================================

\subsection{Background and motivation}

Flexible job shop scheduling with transportation usually takes the transport time of a job between two machines as a constant indexed by location pairs~\cite{xin2025review,meng2025cp,qin2026knowledge},
implicitly assuming that vehicles never compete for corridors. This assumption breaks down when a finite fleet shares a sparse corridor network~\cite{kim2026congestion},
so existing work records the intervals in which each vehicle occupies each corridor in a time-window reservation table before execution and decides machine assignment jointly with conflict-free paths~\cite{vanos2026exact,sun2023integrated,lyu2019approach,li2026framework}.
A schedule then contains, besides machine assignment and processing order, a time-window reservation table recording which vehicle occupies which corridor during which interval.
A disturbance may make operations unexecutable as planned, e.g., a machine failure or a vehicle failure;
or it may invalidate only some corridor occupancies in the reservation table without changing any operation, e.g., a corridor being blocked or slowed down for some period.

These works answer how to build a conflict-free schedule before execution, when information is complete. Once the shop has executed up to time \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) and a disturbance occurs, three questions arise:
which corridor occupancies the disturbance invalidates; which occupancies must be rewritten so that the schedule again satisfies corridor exclusivity and the closure constraints under the disturbance and can thus continue to be executed;
and whether such an executable schedule can be obtained within milliseconds of computation time without restarting a population-based search.

\subsection{The gap the operation-precedence graph cannot capture}

%% Narrowed wording (2026-08-16): the original sentence ``the local rescheduling literature commonly
%% delineates the affected region on the task-dependency graph'' was a universal negative about a whole
%% literature that a single counterexample refutes---railway alternative graphs and MAPF do not delineate
%% on the operation-precedence graph. Narrowed to ``the strand of dynamic scheduling of flexible job shops
%% with transportation''; see Section 2.5.
In dynamic scheduling of flexible job shops with transportation, the rescheduling scope is usually delineated on the operation-precedence graph.
Specifically, the operations that can no longer be executed as planned because of an equipment failure serve as seeds; the operations that follow the seeds within the same job,
and the operations processed after the seed operations on the same machine, are added to the scope. Alternatively, the expansion may proceed from the seeds along the graph for at most \(\theta\) steps,
where \(\theta\) is a manually set upper bound on the expansion depth that keeps the rescheduling scope near the seeds and prevents it from spreading along dependency chains over the whole schedule.
Only the operations in this scope are then rescheduled, and all other operations keep the original plan.
In the existing literature, dynamic scheduling studies with AGV transport and with machine failures or rush orders as disturbances delineate the scope in this way. Their recovery scopes are, respectively, ``the operations not yet processed after the failure''~\cite{zhang2023energy}
and ``the operations and delivery tasks classified by disturbance impact into retained, continued, and reconstructed''~\cite{tang2026dynamic} (the latter studies a hybrid flow shop with lot streaming whose disturbances include vehicle failures, but its model has no reservation table).
This practice can be traced back to affected-operation rescheduling (AOR)~\cite{zhang2023energy,tang2026dynamic}.
In what follows, a baseline that delineates the scope on the operation-precedence graph represents this practice (Section~\ref{sec:rw_aor}).

This way of delineating the scope is often reasonable when the disturbance itself is an operation event, e.g., a machine failure that prevents several operations from being executed under the original assignment.
When the schedule records corridor occupancies with time windows, however, there is another class of disturbances: those that make a corridor impassable for some period without disabling any machine or operation. A typical example is corridor blockage,
in which a corridor is closed to traffic during \([t_a,t_b)\). A blockage modifies neither machine assignment nor processing order, and machines and operations remain available,
so the seed set of AOR---the operations that can no longer be executed as planned because of the disturbance---is empty, and expansion along the operation-precedence graph has nowhere to start;
the delineated scope is empty, and the method concludes that no rescheduling is needed.
Yet the planned corridor occupancies that overlap the blockage interval conflict with the closure, so the original schedule is infeasible.
Moreover, a corridor is an exclusive resource that admits only one vehicle at a time, so a vehicle planned to traverse the same corridor later must wait for the preceding vehicle to leave; we call this precedence a yielding relation.
When the blocked vehicle is delayed by waiting or detouring, the vehicle planned to traverse the corridor after it is delayed in turn, which then delays its own follower, so the invalidation propagates vehicle by vehicle across the reservation table along yielding relations.

``Cannot capture'' in the title of this subsection means the following: the blockage itself is known, so the problem is not detection; rather, the operation-precedence graph on which AOR delineates the scope has operations as vertices and job and machine precedences as edges,
and by definition contains neither corridor occupancies nor their yielding relations, so it can express neither the occupancy invalidation nor its propagation between vehicles.
If transport times are still taken from a constant matrix indexed by location pairs, corridors are not contested resources and no reservation table exists, so this kind of invalidation cannot even be expressed in the model;
the gap therefore arises only in settings that record corridor occupancies with time windows.

Accordingly, we distinguish two classes of disturbances.
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=0.25em]
\item \textbf{Class A} (acting on the operation-precedence graph): robotic-arm failures, significant deviations in processing time, rush-order insertions, and \emph{vehicle failures}.
These disturbances prevent some operations from being executed as planned, so the seed operation set used to delineate the rescheduling scope is nonempty;
scoping on the operation-precedence graph and scoping on the reservation impact set both yield nonempty scopes, so this class is not the main object for testing the scope mismatch.
The reason for placing vehicle failures in Class A is given in Section~\ref{sec:disturbance_classes}: although a vehicle failure changes no operation assignment,
it makes the operations carried by that vehicle unexecutable, so the mapping from a vehicle to the operations it carries still yields a nonempty seed operation set on the operation-precedence graph.
\item \textbf{Class B} (acting only on the reservation table): corridor blockage, corridor slowdown, temporary revocation of local right of way, and the like.
These disturbances disable no machine or operation, so at the operation level no operation appears affected and the seed operation set on the operation-precedence graph is empty;
yet the set of corridor occupancies that overlap the disturbed interval is nonempty, and so is the reservation impact set obtained by taking them as seeds.
\end{itemize}
Class B disturbances are a problem-level counterexample to scoping on the operation-precedence graph: when a model captures processing order with the operation-precedence graph
and records corridor occupancies with time windows, a rescheduling scope delineated only on the operation-precedence graph cannot represent disturbances such as corridor blockage.
The existing dynamic scheduling studies with AGVs cited above do not consider corridor contention among vehicles (Section~\ref{sec:rw_position}),
so the operation-precedence graph is the only scope object available to them; the mismatch arises only when both kinds of objects are present (Section~\ref{sec:rw_claim}).
Figure~\ref{fig:motivate} illustrates the gap. In panel~(a), the scope is delineated on the operation-precedence graph, which contains no seed operation, so no rescheduling is deemed necessary;
in panel~(b), the scope is delineated on the reservation table by the spatiotemporal reservation closure proposed in this paper (Section~\ref{sec:strc}): the occupancies that overlap the blockage interval become seeds,
and subsequent occupancies are added step by step along yielding relations and succession relations (the next occupancy of the same vehicle, of the same job, and on the same machine), forming the reservation impact set.

