In summary, in the flexible job shop with conflict-free AGV routing, a schedule uses operation precedence to describe processing order
and time windows to record corridor occupancy. The situation described in the previous subsection then arises:
a disturbance such as a corridor blockage does not act directly on machines or operations, and at the operation level neither the machine assignment nor the processing sequence needs to change;
yet the corridor occupancies overlapping the disturbed interval are invalidated, so the original schedule is infeasible, and the resulting transport delays also postpone the start of downstream operations.
As Table~\ref{tab:position} shows, none of the listed works combines all three settings---operation and machine assignment, occupancy time windows, and execution-time disturbances:
railway scheduling and online MAPF, which record occupancy time windows and handle execution-time disturbances, have no operation and machine assignment,
whereas the works that have operation and machine assignment and handle execution-time disturbances do not record occupancy time windows and therefore do not handle corridor disturbances.
With all three settings present, this paper moves the rescheduling scope onto corridor occupancies and their yielding and succession relations, which yields a reservation impact set uniquely determined by these relations.

The last columns of the table also state the principle by which this paper delineates the scope. The size of the scope puts ``no misses'' first: every invalidated occupancy must be included,
otherwise the repaired schedule is still infeasible; subject to this, occupancies unreachable along the propagation channels are excluded and keep their original plan,
but the impact set is not meant to be minimal, because a minimal set depends on the repair actions used (Remark~\ref{rem:minimality}).
Nor are more propagation channels necessarily better; the channels must match those along which invalidation actually propagates. Omitting one class misses the occupancies affected through that class of resource---
AOR expands only along jobs and machines and therefore obtains an empty scope under a corridor blockage---while including relations that do not propagate invalidation only enlarges the scope needlessly.
The four channels of this paper---corridor (yielding), vehicle, job, and machine---correspond to the four classes of shared resources that constrain the timing of corridor occupancies in the model.
The objects inside the scope are corridor occupancies: their routes and time intervals may be changed (and, under a vehicle failure, their vehicles as well), while the machine assignment and processing sequence of operations stay unchanged;
if repair fails, the scope is enlarged in a prescribed order (Section~\ref{sec:repair}).

