\subsection{System setting and notation}
\label{sec:system_brief}

The problem studied here belongs to flexible job shop scheduling with conflict-free transportation constraints,
so we build our model on the formulation of this problem by van Os and Basten~\cite{vanos2026exact}; the two differ in what is reserved (Section~\ref{sec:reservation}).
This subsection introduces the objects and notation shared by the later sections.
The feasible pre-disturbance schedule \(\mathcal{S}^0\) is taken as a given input:
the delineation in later sections depends only on \(\mathcal{S}^0\) itself (machine assignment, processing sequence, and reservation table),
not on the algorithm that produced it at \(t=0\), so \(\mathcal{S}^0\) may come from any scheduling method.

\subsubsection{Shop floor and four groups of decisions}
\label{sec:shop}

The shop routing graph \(G=(V,E)\) is strongly connected. Each machine \(m\) performs processing through a robotic arm located at a fixed node \(\mathrm{loc}(m)\);
a robotic-arm failure below means the failure of that machine.
\(v_0\in V\) is the load/unload station. There are \(n\) jobs, a machine set \(M\), and a homogeneous fleet of unit-load vehicles
\(K=\{1,\ldots,N_A\}\). Job \(j\) has a fixed chain of operations \(O_{j1}\to\cdots\to O_{jn_j}\);
operation \(O_{ji}\) has a candidate machine set \(\Omega_{ji}\subseteq M\) and processing time
\(t^P_{jim}\) on machine \(m\). A complete schedule fixes four groups of decisions simultaneously:
(i)~the machine assignment \(x\) of each operation;
(ii)~the processing sequence \(\pi\) of operations on each machine;
(iii)~the vehicle assignment \(y\) of each transport;
(iv)~a conflict-free path on \(G\) for each vehicle move.
We write
\[
\mathcal{S}=(x,\,\pi,\,y,\,R),
\]
where the reservation table \(R\) is the explicit record of group (iv), defined below.

Transport tasks are not fixed parameters given in advance; they are determined by the machine assignment \(x\):
a transport is needed only when two consecutive operations of the same job are assigned to machines at different nodes,
and when the assignment changes, the number of transports and their origin and destination nodes change accordingly.
Each transport is split into two transport legs. In the \emph{empty leg}, the vehicle travels unloaded from its current position to the node of the machine of the preceding operation to pick up the job;
in the \emph{loaded leg}, it carries the job from that node to the node of the machine of the succeeding operation.
The two legs have their own task labels \(u\) and are two separate records in the reservation table.
Loading and unloading times are zero: the loaded leg departs at the later of ``the empty leg arrives at the pickup node'' and ``the job completes its preceding operation'',
and the delivery time is the time at which the succeeding operation can start and the vehicle can accept a new task.
Later sections decide which occupancies are rewritable, which are kept as planned, and how rerouting is done with a single transport leg (empty or loaded) as the basic unit,
not a whole transport or a whole operation.

\subsubsection{Corridor reservation and conflict freedom}
\label{sec:reservation}

Each corridor \(e\in E\) is an exclusive resource with known traversal time \(\tau_e\). Corridors are treated as single lanes:
a vehicle traveling in either direction occupies the whole corridor, so at most one vehicle is on \(e\) at any time,
and neither head-on encounters nor same-direction overtaking can occur.
This corresponds to the single-lane narrow aisles and zone-based occupancy control common in AGV shops;
a two-lane corridor can be represented as two corridors in the network.
Occupancies are half-open intervals: a following vehicle may enter exactly when the preceding one leaves.
Vehicles wait only at nodes and never stop inside a corridor; nodes have ample parking capacity, so waiting at nodes is unrestricted.
A reservation record is
\[
r=(e,\,[t^s,t^e),\,k,\,u)\in R,
\]
meaning that vehicle \(k\) occupies corridor \(e\) during \([t^s,t^e)\) for leg \(u\).
Any two distinct occupancies of the same corridor must satisfy
\([t^s,t^e)\cap[t^{s\prime},t^{e\prime})=\varnothing\).
When a path is planned, each corridor is used only in time slots not yet occupied in the reservation table \(R\),
so newly written occupancies never overlap existing ones, the reservation table always satisfies the exclusivity constraint above, and no conflict check is needed afterwards.

Time-window routing is the basic module shared by our rerouting and by all baseline methods: given the origin, the destination, the earliest departure time, and the current reservation table \(R\),
it returns a path whose occupancy intervals on all corridors overlap no existing occupancy, together with the entry time of each corridor.
We implement it as a time-window Dijkstra algorithm~\cite{sun2023integrated}. We do not modify this module;
we change only \emph{which} transport legs are handed to it for replanning.
Van Os et al.\ reserve nodes~\cite{vanos2026exact}, and Lyu et al.\ additionally impose node-capacity constraints~\cite{lyu2019approach};
we reserve only corridors (the edges of the network) and place no reservations on nodes,
so our results cannot be compared directly with the values reported in these two works (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsubsection{Operation-precedence graph and reservation dependency graph}
\label{sec:two_graphs}

% selfcheck:allow-term ``task graph'' is a synonym retained by ruling B13, and also explains the subscript of \(G_T\)
The operation-precedence graph \(G_T=(T_T,E_T)\) (also called the task graph in the literature; the subscript \(T\) stands for task) has operations as vertices;
its directed edges represent precedence constraints between two operations and are of two kinds:
\emph{job precedence}, from operation \(O_{ji}\) of a job to its next operation \(O_{j,i+1}\);
and \emph{same-machine precedence}, from an operation on a machine to the operation processed immediately after it on that machine.
\(G_T\) contains no corridor occupancies and therefore does \emph{not} contain the yielding relations between them.
The reservation dependency graph \(G_R=(R,E_R)\) has the corridor occupancies in the reservation table \(R\) as vertices;
a directed edge \(r\to r'\) means that invalidating \(r\) may force \(r'\) to be rerouted, and there are four kinds (Definition~\ref{def:dep}):
\emph{yield} edges between two consecutive occupancies of the same corridor, \emph{same-vehicle} edges between two adjacent occupancies of the same vehicle,
and \emph{same-job} and \emph{same-machine} edges, obtained by carrying the job precedence and same-machine precedence of \(G_T\) over to transport occupancies.
Here the \emph{transport occupancies} of an operation are the corridor occupancies produced by the transport legs that move the job for that operation.
The vertex sets of the two graphs are disjoint: one represents processing, the other traversal.
Hence there is a class of disturbances that changes only when corridors are passable and no operation parameter (e.g., a corridor blockage):
scoping on \(G_T\) finds no seed operation,
whereas on \(G_R\), as long as some planned corridor occupancy overlaps the disturbed interval (exactly the case in which the original schedule becomes infeasible),
these occupancies form a nonempty seed set from which delineation can start.
Figure~\ref{fig:graphs} contrasts the two graphs, Figure~\ref{fig:motivate} shows the consequences under a corridor blockage, and
Table~\ref{tab:notation} summarizes the notation used in this section and later.

