\section{The spatiotemporal reservation closure}
\label{sec:strc}
%% =====================================================================

This section determines the rewritable occupancy set \(U\). Given the reservation table \(R\) and the seed set \(\mathrm{Src}(\mathcal{D})\)
(Definition~\ref{def:seeds}), we build a dependency graph over occupancies and take all occupancies reachable from the seeds along its edges, i.e., \(U=\mathrm{Cl}(\mathrm{Src})\).
How vehicles are rerouted inside \(U\) is described in Section~\ref{sec:repair}.

\subsection{Reservation dependency edges}
\label{sec:closure_def}

\begin{definition}[Reservation dependency graph]
\label{def:dep}
The vertices are the corridor occupancies in \(R\). A directed edge \(r\to r'\) means: once \(r\) is rewritten, \(r'\) may have to be rewritten as well. The edges are of four kinds:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.4em,itemsep=0.15em]
\item \textbf{Yield edges:} two occupancies of the same corridor by different vehicles whose time intervals overlap or meet end to start (\(r.t^e=r'.t^s\));
the edge points from the one with the earlier entry time \(t^s\) to the one with the later entry time;
\item \textbf{Same-vehicle successors:} two occupancies of the same vehicle that are adjacent in time, from the earlier to the later;
\item \textbf{Same-job successors:} from the transport occupancies of operation \(i\) of a job to the transport occupancies of operation \(i+1\);
\item \textbf{Same-machine successors:} for two operations processed consecutively on the same machine, from the transport occupancies of the former to those of the latter.
\end{enumerate}
This yields \(G_R=(R,E_R)\).
\end{definition}

Figure~\ref{fig:graphs}(b) illustrates the four kinds of edges. Two occupancies that meet end to start do not conflict under exclusive semantics,
but once the former is delayed the latter must give way, so they are also joined by a yield edge.
The edge set is a modeling choice: it may include occupancies that need not be rewritten, and it may miss dependencies that are not modeled (Remark~\ref{rem:minimality}).

\begin{definition}[Reservation impact set and spatiotemporal reservation closure]
\label{def:strc}
Given a seed set \(S\subseteq R\) and a time \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\), denote the occupancies unfinished at the decision time by
\[
R^{\circ}=\bigl\{\,r\in R:\ r.t^e>t_{\mathrm{now}}\,\bigr\},
\]
and let \(G_R^\circ\) be the subgraph that keeps only the occupancies in \(R^{\circ}\) and the edges among them. The reservation impact set is defined as
\[
\mathrm{Cl}(S)
=\bigl\{\,v\in R^{\circ}:\
\exists\,s\in S\cap R^{\circ}\ \text{such that}\ s\!\to^*\!v\ \text{in}\ G_R^\circ\,\bigr\},
\]
that is, all unfinished occupancies reachable along edges from the unfinished seeds (the seeds themselves included).
The procedure that computes \(\mathrm{Cl}(S)\) is called the spatiotemporal reservation closure (\STRC).
\end{definition}

With \(G_R^\circ\) stored as adjacency lists, one breadth-first search from the seeds yields \(\mathrm{Cl}(S)\) in
\(O(|R^{\circ}|+|E_R^{\circ}|)\) time, where \(E_R^{\circ}\) is the edge set of \(G_R^\circ\).
The experiments do not time this step separately; it is included in the total runtime reported in Section~\ref{sec:cheap}.

