In the experiments the two are denoted R1 and R2, respectively.

\subsection{Closure}
\label{sec:containment}

\begin{prop}[Structural closure]
\label{prop:struct}
For any \(S\subseteq R\), \(G_R^\circ\) contains no edge
\(u\to v\) with \(u\in\mathrm{Cl}(S)\) and \(v\in R^{\circ}\setminus\mathrm{Cl}(S)\).
Consequently, no unfinished occupancy outside the impact set is reachable along edges from \(\mathrm{Cl}(S)\).
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
Suppose such an edge \(u\to v\) exists. Since \(u\in\mathrm{Cl}(S)\), there is an unfinished seed \(s\) with
\(s\to^* u\). Appending \(u\to v\) gives \(s\to^* v\), so \(v\in\mathrm{Cl}(S)\) by Definition~\ref{def:strc},
a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:struct} is a direct corollary of the definition: once the edge set is fixed, the impact set is closed under these edges.
Whether it covers all dependencies that actually exist physically depends on the choice of edges; this is discussed in the following remark.

\begin{remark}[The reservation impact set and the minimal rewritable set]
\label{rem:minimality}
Closure states only that no dependency edge leads from inside the set to outside it; it neither guarantees that the impact set suffices to restore feasibility nor shows that it is small enough.
We discuss the relation between the impact set and the minimal rewritable set in three points.

First, \textbf{the minimal rewritable set depends on the repair procedure used, whereas the reservation impact set does not}.
To call a set \(U\) minimal, one must first specify ``under which repair capability \(U\) suffices to restore feasibility'': the minimal \(U\) when reassignment and resequencing are allowed
differs from the minimal \(U\) when only rerouting is allowed. The reservation impact set is uniquely determined by \(G_R\) and independent of the repair procedure,
so it cannot, and is not meant to, coincide with the minimal set of any particular repair procedure.

Second, \textbf{the four kinds of edges include occupancies that need not be rewritten; the known case of missed occupancies (meeting end to start) has been folded into the yield edges}.
A yield edge is created whenever two occupancies overlap, without checking whether the latter \emph{truly} can proceed only by waiting for the former---if a bypass exists there,
the latter can simply detour and need not be rewritten; likewise, same-vehicle successor edges may pull in chain tails that the delay cannot reach. Both effects make the impact set larger than necessary.
To test the treatment of occupancies that meet end to start, we run a \emph{difference probe}: on the same \(\NPairs\) instance pairs as Section~\ref{sec:e3},
each occupancy in the impact set is delayed alone by \(1\) time unit, all else unchanged, and we check whether it newly overlaps an occupancy outside the impact set on the same corridor.
Each such overlap counts as a leak, i.e., a dependency that the current edge set does not draw but that would cause a conflict after the delay.
The total number of leaks is \ProbeLeaks, and the number of pairs with a leak is \ProbeLeakPairs; in \ProbeOutsidePairs\ of the pairs the impact set leaves some occupancies outside,
and in the rest it already contains all unfinished occupancies. This test targets only the known omission of occupancies meeting end to start and does not prove the edge set complete.

Third, \textbf{empirically, the impact set is not small}. Under corridor blockage, the reservation impact set covers on average about \ClosureAliveFrac\ of the occupancies
unfinished after \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) (Section~\ref{sec:e6}); compared with the maximal choice of rewriting all unfinished occupancies, it leaves out less than one tenth.
The savings of bounded repair come mainly from \emph{not rewriting finished occupancies} and \emph{not restarting the search},
not from a sharp reduction of the future occupancies to be rewritten; ``local'' and ``bounded'' do not mean that the changes are small.
This ``less than one tenth'' refers to the size of the rewritable set, not to the fraction actually rewritten. The comparison in Section~\ref{sec:cheap} shows
that this very difference lowers the rewrite fraction markedly, while runtime and makespan remain nearly unchanged.

What this paper provides, therefore, is a rewritable set that is uniquely determined by structure, reproducible, and closed under the dependency edges,
and that sufficed to restore feasibility in all our experiments; approximating the minimal rewritable set is a separate problem, left for future work.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Can the impact-set size be predicted from the network structure?}
\label{sec:structure}

The size of the reservation impact set is uniquely determined by \(G_R\): once the schedule and network are given, there is no tunable parameter.
When the instances are scaled from \(8\times4\times4\) up to \(\ScaleJobsHi\times\ScaleMachHi\times\ScaleMachHi\),
the share of unfinished occupancies covered by the impact set stays between \ScaleCloAliveLo\ and \ScaleCloAliveHi\ and does not rise with size (Section~\ref{sec:instscale}).
Hence the extent of ``local'' is measurable, but its size is not small (Remark~\ref{rem:minimality}).

The impact-set size is determined jointly by the schedule and the network. The experiments examine indicators computed from the network alone and independent of the schedule
(the load/unload minimum cut and the funnel share) and find that they do not predict the impact-set size: on external networks these indicators are constant,
whereas the impact-set share changes markedly when the machine configuration on the same network is changed (Section~\ref{sec:e4}).

