all unfinished occupancies, and the rerouting is retried. Bottom row: two baselines,
one scoping by the affected set on the operation-precedence graph and one performing a warm-started global search; the three paths feed the same set of output metrics.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Single-pass rerouting that keeps the rest as planned}
\label{sec:level1}

Given the rewritable occupancy set \(U\), single-pass rerouting keeps the original vehicle and machine assignments and reschedules the transports in \(U\) one by one in the original processing order.
Rerouting works per occupancy: the part of a trip that a vehicle has already traversed before \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) consists of finished occupancies, which are pre-committed unchanged,
and rerouting plans only from the node where the vehicle is at that time.
Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay} gives this basic operation; Algorithm~\ref{alg:strc_repair} (Section~\ref{sec:scope_escalation})
wraps it, computing the reservation impact set and escalating \(U\) on failure.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Single-pass rerouting \(\textsc{ReplayReroute}\)}
\label{alg:replay}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require schedule \(\mathcal{S}^0=(x,\pi,y,R)\), disturbance \(\mathcal{D}\), rewritable set \(U\), time \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\)
\Ensure schedule \(\mathcal{S}'\) or failure
\State write \(\mathcal{D}\) into the routing layer
  \Comment{blockage: write the blockage window; slowdown: traversal time inside the window lengthened by a factor}
\State take an empty table \(R'\)
\For{each occupancy not in \(U\) (including all finished occupancies)}
  \State pre-commit the original fields \((e,[t^s,t^e),k)\) to \(R'\)
    \Comment{finished occupancies must be written unchanged; omitting them amounts to rewriting them}
  \If{conflict with the blockage window}
    \State \Return failure
      \Comment{the seeds do not cover an occupancy overlapping the blockage window}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\For{each \(r\in U\), in the original processing order}
  \State plan from the node where the vehicle is at \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) \Comment{the traversed part was pre-committed in the previous step and is not committed again}
  \State invoke time-window routing on the current \(R'\) and commit the new occupancies
  \State update the idle time of the machine and the ready time of the job concerned according to \(\pi\)
\EndFor
\State independent check: no conflicts, no occupancy inside a blockage window, traversal times inside slowdown windows lengthened, fields outside the set unchanged
\If{the check passes}
  \State \Return \(\mathcal{S}'=(x,\pi,y,R')\)
\Else
  \State \Return failure
\EndIf
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

By complexity, delineating the scope needs only one breadth-first search (Definition~\ref{def:strc}), whereas one call of
\(\textsc{ReplayReroute}\) invokes a time-window shortest path once for each transport leg in \(U\); the runtime comes mainly from the latter.
Rerouting launches no population-based search. The comparison in Section~\ref{sec:e3} fixes Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay} and replaces only the source of \(U\)
(the operation-precedence graph or the reservation impact set), so that a difference in feasibility rate between the two scopings can come only from the scope object.

\begin{prop}[A sufficient condition for unchanged fields outside the set]
\label{prop:outside}
Let the rewritable occupancy set be \(U=\mathrm{Cl}(S)\), let single-pass rerouting be executed by Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay}, and suppose:
(i)~the blockage windows have been written into the routing layer; (ii)~for every \(r\in R^{\circ}\setminus U\), its original fields \((e,[t^s,t^e),k)\)
have been pre-committed to the new reservation table, and the pre-commit succeeds; (iii)~the time-window router replans only the occupancies in \(U\);
(iv)~the final schedule passes the conflict-free check.
Then, in the recovered reservation table \(R'\), the corridor, interval, and vehicle of every \(r\in R^{\circ}\setminus U\) agree with \(\mathcal{S}^0\).
The proposition holds per occupancy, not per whole transport: a transport may have some occupancies in \(U\)
and others outside it, in which case only the part outside \(U\) is protected by this proposition.
\end{prop}

\begin{proof}
By (ii), every occupancy outside the set is written into the new table with its original interval before the replay begins, and it does not conflict with the blockage windows
(otherwise the pre-commit would fail). By (iii), the replay commits new occupancies only for occupancies in \(U\) and neither deletes nor rewrites the committed occupancies outside the set.
Hence every occupancy outside the set has the same \((e,[t^s,t^e),k)\) in the new table as its pre-committed value, i.e., as in \(\mathcal{S}^0\).
The check in (iv) only rules out conflicts between new and existing occupancies; it does not alter committed fields.
\end{proof}

Premises (i)--(iv) are execution conditions of the rerouting procedure itself and can be checked one by one in the implementation; beyond them, the proposition relies on
A1 (the initial schedule is feasible; otherwise the original fields outside the set might conflict with one another and the pre-commit would necessarily fail),
A2 (finished occupancies are not revisited), and A4 (corridors are exclusive).

\begin{remark}[When the premises of Proposition~\ref{prop:outside} fail]
\label{rem:precommit}
There are three cases.
First, a failed pre-commit: if an occupancy outside the set overlaps a blockage window, premise (ii) fails,
which means that the seed set \(S\) does not cover all occupancies overlapping the blocked interval, i.e., the seed set is incomplete.
Second, scope escalation: if rerouting fails and triggers the scope escalation of Section~\ref{sec:scope_escalation},
the rewritable set becomes \(U'\supsetneq U\), and the proposition holds only for the occupancies that finally remain outside the set.
Escalation gives priority to feasibility rather than to minimal rewriting; escalations are reported separately in the experiments.
Third, too coarse an implementation granularity: premise (iii) requires the time-window router to replan \emph{only} the occupancies in \(U\).
If an implementation decides whether to reroute at a unit coarser than an occupancy (e.g., a whole transport or a whole operation), occupancies outside \(U\) are rewritten along with it,
and the conclusion of the proposition fails. The implementation must therefore decide rerouting per occupancy.
The granularity must in fact go down to segments: for a transport leg spanning \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\), the segments already traversed are kept as they are,
and rerouting starts from the node where the vehicle is at \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\); otherwise finished occupancies are rewritten, violating Assumption A2.
Because these segments lie inside \(U\), a comparison of the fields outside the set cannot detect such rewrites.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Scope escalation after failure}
\label{sec:scope_escalation}

Escalating the scope step by step after failure has precedents. Local Rescheduling~\cite{kuster2007local}
relaxes the time window step by step until feasibility is reached; AOR adds subsequent operations along same-job and same-machine successors
(Section~\ref{sec:rw_aor}). We escalate \(U\) along reservation dependency edges rather than along time windows.
This step ensures that feasibility can still be restored when the impact set is too tight, without switching immediately to a global search.
E3 disables this step in order to compare the feasibility rates of the two scopings (Section~\ref{sec:e3}).

With all occupancies outside \(U\) fixed, if the occupancies in \(U\) find no feasible path and interval (e.g., a transport cannot arrive when its operation requires),
Algorithm~\ref{alg:replay} returns failure. We then escalate \(U\) step by step in the following order and retry the same rerouting procedure; this is called \emph{scope escalation} below:
(i)~\(U\leftarrow U\cup\{\)the unfinished occupancies in the same-job suffixes (all later transport occupancies of the job) of the operations currently in \(U\}\);
(ii)~\(U\leftarrow U\cup\{\)the unfinished occupancies in the same-vehicle suffixes (all later occupancies of the vehicle) of the vehicles owning occupancies in \(U\}\);
(iii)~\(U\leftarrow\{\,r\in R:\ r.t^e>t_{\mathrm{now}}\,\}\), i.e., every occupancy unfinished at the decision time becomes rewritable;
this is exactly the rewritable set of the baseline RA (Section~\ref{sec:vs_r0}).
After escalation, rerouting remains a deterministic single pass. In the experiments this step restored feasibility in all instance--seed pairs,
with runtime staying in the range of milliseconds to a little over ten milliseconds (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).

Algorithm~\ref{alg:strc_repair} combines Section~\ref{sec:level1} and this section, corresponding to Figure~\ref{fig:framework}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Bounded repair on the reservation impact set (single-pass rerouting + scope escalation)}
\label{alg:strc_repair}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require schedule \(\mathcal{S}^0\), disturbance \(\mathcal{D}\), time \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\)
\Ensure schedule \(\mathcal{S}'\) or failure
\State \(S \gets \mathrm{Src}(\mathcal{D})\);
      \(U \gets \mathrm{Cl}(S)\)
\For{round \(=0,1,2,3\)}
  \State \(\mathcal{S}' \gets \textsc{ReplayReroute}(\mathcal{S}^0,\mathcal{D},U,t_{\mathrm{now}})\)
  \If{\(\mathcal{S}'\) is feasible}
    \State \Return \(\mathcal{S}'\)
  \EndIf
  \State update \(U\) by the scope-escalation rule \Comment{job suffix / same-vehicle suffix / all unfinished}
\EndFor
\State \Return failure
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:vs_r0}

The global rescheduling baseline (denoted R0+) searches anew under the same no-entry constraints and within a time limit.
R0+ is a genetic algorithm that encodes \((x,\pi)\) as a chromosome. One \emph{decoding} means: following the chromosome's assignments and processing order,
invoke the time-window routing of Section~\ref{sec:reservation} from scratch for all transports on the reservation table into which the blockage windows have been written,
obtaining a new \(R\) and \(C_{\max}\). R0+ decodes repeatedly and may modify the chromosome.
Its decoding does not rewrite finished occupancies: these are pre-committed, and only operations not yet started are scheduled from \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) on (Section~\ref{sec:e5}).
R0+ is warm-started, i.e., the original schedule is placed in the initial population as one individual, so as not to discard the assignment and path information already in it.
Because R0+ can reassign and resequence, it is more likely to shorten \(C_{\max}\), but its runtime is set by the budget;
the two methods are compared in makespan and runtime in Section~\ref{sec:e5}.

Comparing only with R0+ leaves one question open: within a millisecond runtime, is scoping on the impact set necessary?
To answer it, we implement three approaches that do not restrict the scope by the impact set. Global right shift (RS) does not determine which occupancies
are affected; it simply pushes all unfinished occupancies past the end of the blockage. Re-decoding of the original chromosome (RD) leaves the chromosome unchanged:
the finished part is kept, and the rest is decoded once from the original chromosome.
\textbf{RA} makes every occupancy still unfinished after \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) rewritable and then runs \emph{exactly the same} rerouting procedure as in Section~\ref{sec:level1}.
It therefore differs from our method only in ``which occupancies are rewritable'' and is the only baseline that isolates the effect of the reservation impact set.
All three run within milliseconds and thus serve as baselines for bounded repair \emph{within the same runtime range};
their definitions and results are given in Section~\ref{sec:cheap}.

