\subsection{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:protocol}

\textbf{Network, routing, and instances.}
The lower-level network, the time-window router, and the independent checker are fixed. The extended experiment matrix uses \textbf{five instances}, with size written as ``jobs \(\times\) machines \(\times\) AGVs'':
a small \(3\times3\times2\) instance for step-by-step checking, a congested \(8\times4\times4\) instance,
and three \(8\times4\times4\) spine layouts, funnel, high, and mid.
The first two are hand-crafted; the last three are produced by our instance generator, whose parameters include the congestion level, heterogeneity \(h\), and flexibility \(f\).
In a spine layout, a main corridor (the spine) runs through the machine area and each machine hangs on one of two hubs of the spine via its own branch;
the load/unload station sits at one end of the spine and is connected by one or two parallel exits~\cite{suh2022spine}.
Every transport must pass the same exit and the same middle section, so contention concentrates on these few corridors; the congested instance also has this layout.
The three spine layouts have the same size, heterogeneity, and flexibility and differ only in the number of parallel lanes:
funnel has one lane at the station exit and one in the middle section, high doubles the exit, and mid additionally doubles the middle section.
Adding or removing a lane changes only how many vehicles can pass at once, not the travel distance, so differences in impact-set size among the three can be attributed to lane capacity rather than to problem size.
The random seed used to generate an instance determines only the instance itself and is independent of the run seeds below, which determine the disturbance injection and the search process.
\textbf{We use ten run seeds}, all fixed once before the experiments and not selected by results: \(42\), \(7\), \(2024\), \(99\), \(123\), \(13\), \(1\),
\(777\), \(31415\), and \(8\); E5 uses the first three of them.
The five instances and ten seeds give \(\NInst\times\NSeeds=\NPairs\) instance--seed pairs (``pairs'' for short below).
All per-pair comparisons, win/loss counts, and paired tests in this paper use the instance--seed pair as the unit:
in the same pair, all methods read the same initial solution, the same blockage, and the same decision time.
The initial schedule is obtained by replaying the time-window router of Section~\ref{sec:reservation} in processing order,
and it passes the independent conflict-free check; all baseline methods share this initial solution within the same instance--seed pair.
We require only that \(\mathcal{S}^0\) be feasible before the disturbance.

\textbf{Implementation and computing environment.}
All experiments are implemented in pure Python with the standard library only, without third-party numerical libraries; the lower-level network, the time-window router, and the independent checker
reuse the same implementation as our static integrated work. Everything runs on a single machine in a single thread, without parallelism: Intel Xeon W-1270
(\(8\) cores, \(16\) threads), \(64\)\,GB RAM, Windows 10, and the CPython \(3.7.6\) interpreter (\(64\)-bit).
All timing results were obtained under the single-machine, single-thread setting above.
Different hardware would shift them uniformly, so all our runtime conclusions are stated as \emph{ratios} or \emph{orders of magnitude} and do not rely on absolute milliseconds.

\textbf{Second batch: layouts from the published literature.}
The five instances above use layouts we constructed; the three spine layouts have the same node positions and route lengths and differ only in the number of parallel lanes, i.e., in how many vehicles can pass at once.
To test whether the conclusions depend on these particular layouts, we run an additional batch of \NPubInst\ instances. The grid size, the nodes of the load/unload station and of each machine, and which adjacent node pairs of the grid have no corridor between them
are transcribed item by item from the layout figures in Appendix~A of Lyu et al.~\cite{lyu2019approach}, and the transcription is checked item by item by a script against the machine-readable encoding
shipped with the van Os toolkit. The number of machines varies with the layout, from \(4\) to \(8\).
The same ten random seeds are used, giving \NPubPairs\ instance--seed pairs in total. The two batches share the number of jobs,
the number of AGVs, the number of operations per job, \(h\), \(f\), and the calibration target of the transport-to-processing time ratio,
so any difference between the two batches can come only from the layouts themselves.

That appendix gives six layouts in order (called layouts 1--6 below), with \(3\) to \(8\) machines respectively. We fix the flexibility at \(f=0.6\) for all instances;
with only \(3\) machines, each operation then has on average only \(1.8\) candidate machines, short of the requirement that ``every operation has at least two candidate machines''.
Relaxing \(f\) for this single layout would introduce an extra variable, so layout 1 is not used.

The remaining five layouts \textbf{fall on only \NPubNets\ distinct networks}. Layouts 2 and 3 (\(4\) and \(5\) machines) share the same \(4\times 4\) grid:
\(16\) nodes, where adjacent nodes would give \(24\) corridors, but in the layout figure one pair of adjacent nodes has no corridor between them, leaving \(23\).
Layouts 4--6 (\(6\), \(7\), and \(8\) machines) share the same \(5\times 5\) grid: \(25\) nodes, \(40\) corridors in a full grid,
two of which are absent, leaving \(38\). The five layouts differ in the number and positions of machines, not in network shape.
Therefore, when Section~\ref{sec:e4} analyzes this batch,
\textbf{the number of independent samples along the network-shape dimension is \NPubNets, not \NPubInst}, whereas along the machine-configuration dimension (number and positions)
it is \NPubInst; all claims below are stated according to this division.

This batch differs from the data published by Lyu et al.\ in three respects. \textbf{First, the per-edge travel times are filled in by us.}
Lyu et al.\ give per-edge durations only for a single example instance (and they are not uniform); the Appendix~A layouts come without per-edge durations.
We fill them in with ``all edges equally weighted,'' which is consistent with the ``constant unit-step duration'' assumption of the van Os model and differs only in the scale of the time unit.
Consequently, this batch is not compared numerically with the values reported by Lyu or van Os; we borrow only their networks.
\textbf{Second, the loading and unloading stations are merged into one load/unload station.} The original layouts have separate loading and unloading stations;
we follow the single load/unload station setting common in the joint machine--AGV scheduling literature~\cite{fontes2023hybrid} (Section~\ref{sec:problem}),
in which jobs depart from the same station \(v_0\) and return to it after completion. We therefore take the loading station at the vehicles' starting location as \(v_0\) and keep the original unloading station as an ordinary grid node.
The network itself (grid, adjacent node pairs without a corridor, and the node of each machine) is unchanged; only the endpoints of each job's first and last transport change.
\textbf{Third, their job data are not used.} In the public instance families we have measured, the average transport time is only a few percent of the average
processing time, so transport hardly causes any contention; if their job data were also adopted, corridor contention and all
phenomena that depend on it would become unobservable. Hence this batch takes only their networks, and the job data are generated with the same parameters as the first batch.

The van Os toolkit also contains a set of layouts labeled Liu 2023, which is not included in this batch. These layouts allow vehicles to move diagonally,
whereas our network contains only corridors between nodes adjacent up, down, left, or right (Section~\ref{sec:problem}); the two network models differ, so these layouts are not used.

\textbf{Disturbances.}
All experiments use the same decision time \(t_{\mathrm{now}}=0.35\,C_{\max}(\mathcal{S}^0)\). This value was fixed before the experiments: at this time about \(40\%\) of the occupancies have finished and \(60\%\) are still unfinished,
so there is both an executed part that must stay unchanged and a sufficiently large part to repair; we did not perform a sensitivity analysis on this value. The disturbances are as follows.
E1--E3, E5, and E7 block one busy corridor, namely the corridor with the most occupancies still unfinished at \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\);
the blockage is one continuous time window that runs from the earliest start to the latest end of these occupancies and therefore covers all of them.
We call this disturbance a \emph{busy-corridor blockage} below.
E4 blocks the corridors in the load/unload minimum cut (Section~\ref{sec:e4}). E6 adds three further disturbance types at the same decision time (Section~\ref{sec:e6}).
The disturbance-magnitude sweep first sorts the operations that still have unfinished occupancies at \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) by the start time of their earliest such occupancy
and takes the first fraction \(\varphi\) as disturbed operations; each unfinished occupancy of these operations then generates its own blockage, whose corridor and interval are exactly those of the occupancy.
The blockages are not merged into one long window, since a merged window would also cover the occupancies of unselected operations lying in between.

\textbf{Baseline methods.}
Six methods are compared, listed in increasing order of their difference from our method:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em,itemsep=0.2em]
\item \textbf{R2 (our method, \STRC)}: scoping on the reservation impact set, followed by single-pass rerouting.
Scope escalation is enabled only in the magnitude sweep and in E8, and disabled in E1--E3, E5, and E7.
\item \textbf{R1}: scoping on the affected set of the operation-precedence graph, followed by the same single-pass rerouting as R2, with \(\theta=2\) throughout.
\(\theta\) is the maximum number of hops of forward expansion along the within-job operation precedence on the operation-precedence graph; it affects only \(|U_{R1}|\) under Class A disturbances.
A Class B disturbance acts directly on no operation, so the seed operation set is empty and the result is empty however many hops are expanded (Proposition~\ref{prop:miss}).
R1 and R2 differ only in the scope object.
\item \textbf{RA}: no restriction to the impact set; all occupancies still unfinished after \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) are made rewritable,
and the rerouting procedure is identical to R2, so that the role of the reservation impact set is tested in isolation (Section~\ref{sec:cheap}).
\item \textbf{RS}: global right shift. It neither scopes nor reroutes and simply pushes all unfinished occupancies past the end of the blockage (Section~\ref{sec:cheap}).
\item \textbf{RD}: re-decoding of the original chromosome. The chromosome is unchanged and decoded once with a fixed prefix; this is the cheapest global recomputation in our implementation (Section~\ref{sec:cheap}).
\item \textbf{R0+}: global rescheduling that searches again within a time limit, starting from the original schedule. It is a genetic algorithm with population size \(40\),
with the chromosome of the original schedule seeded into the initial population; it uses the same fixed-prefix decoding as RD, so occupancies before \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) are not rewritable.
\emph{No generation limit or early-stopping threshold is set}; it terminates only on the wall-clock budget, whose values are given in Section~\ref{sec:e5}.
\end{itemize}
The five baselines differ from R2 by successively larger steps: R1 only in the scope \emph{object}, RA only in \emph{whether} the scope is delineated,
RS neither delineates nor reroutes, and RD and R0+ recompute globally and differ only in how many generations they search.

The scoping rule of R1 is taken from the operation-level
local rescheduling approach described in Section~\ref{sec:rw_aor} and is reimplemented by us. The correspondence is as follows. The scoping rule ``take the directly disturbed operations as the affected set,
then expand forward along the within-job operation precedence'' corresponds to
AOR~\cite{subramaniam2003maor}.
The two further components, \emph{truncating the expansion at \(\theta\) hops} and \emph{mapping the operation set to the corridor reservations to be released},
are specified by us---the original setting has no reservation table, so this mapping has no counterpart there.
The rerouting and replay components share the same implementation as R2, so that the two differ only in the scope object.
R1 is therefore a baseline that transplants this scoping rule into our reservation setting; its results reflect how the rule performs in this setting,
not how the original method performs in its own setting.

\textbf{Budget convention.}
The methods are not compared at the same budget level. R1, R2, RS, RD, and RA are each a single deterministic repair
that terminates when the run finishes; their runtime is determined by instance size and impact-set size rather than by a budget setting. Only R0+ terminates on a wall-clock budget.
All results involving R0+ are therefore obtained under a given budget; the other five methods each run once and terminate, so they can be compared with one another directly. Wall-clock times are reported per batch and are not compared across batches.

\textbf{Division of labor between the two batches.}
The two batches are not two samples of the same body of evidence; they answer different questions.
The main batch answers \emph{whether the phenomena exist and whether the mechanism can be identified in isolation}: under Class B disturbances, scoping on the operation-precedence graph yields an empty scope while the original schedule is infeasible
(Section~\ref{sec:e1}); the reservation impact set contains that scope and the fields outside the set remain invariant (Section~\ref{sec:e2});
and only the scope object is replaced under the same rerouting procedure (Section~\ref{sec:e3}). All three require full control over the instance parameters,
especially the transport-to-processing time ratio that determines whether corridor contention arises, and the public instance families cannot provide such control. The second batch answers a different question: \emph{whether these phenomena
hold only on the layouts we constructed}. It is used only to test whether the main-batch results can be reproduced on the layouts of Lyu et al.
(see Section~\ref{sec:pubrepro}, ``Reproduction on the external-layout batch'') and yields no new performance-comparison conclusions.

The two batches are analyzed jointly in only two places. The first is E4. The three same-size layouts of the main batch belong to one family with very small geometric differences,
so two explanations---``the indicator is too coarse'' and ``the instances differ too little in geometry''---cannot be distinguished. Conversely, without a baseline on our own layouts matched parameter by parameter,
the external layouts alone could not tell whether the variation in their impact-set share is large enough. The second batch serves to separate these two explanations.
The second is E5: the E5 setup and parameters are identical in both batches, and pooling serves only to increase the sample size.
The conclusions of all other sections are drawn from the main batch alone; the second batch only strengthens their external validity.

\textbf{Statistical convention.}
The paper contains two inferential tests, both in Section~\ref{sec:cheap}: the difference between R2 and RA in rewrite fraction (the primary test)
and the difference between R2 and RD in makespan. Both use the two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test, with the instance--seed pair as the pairing unit, i.e., on the difference between the two methods on the same pair.
After Holm correction for these two tests, the significance conclusion of each is unchanged. All other per-pair win/loss counts, means, and medians
are \emph{descriptive} statistics, and no significance claims are made for them.
The primary test is accompanied by a Hodges--Lehmann effect-size estimate with a \(95\%\) bootstrap confidence interval
(percentile method, \(10^4\) resamples, fixed random seed); dispersion is reported as the interquartile range.
The tables of all other batches give only means, medians, and counts, without intervals.

\subsection{E1: the operation-precedence graph yields an empty scope}
\label{sec:e1}

This section tests whether Proposition~\ref{prop:miss} holds on the extended instances. Following the setup of Section~\ref{sec:protocol},
one busy corridor is blocked in each of the \(\NPairs\) pairs, the scope is delineated both on the operation-precedence graph and by the reservation impact set,
and we check whether the original schedule is still feasible after the blockage. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:e1} and Figure~\ref{fig:e1e3} (left).

Table~\ref{tab:e1} shows that the three conditions of the proposition hold together in all \(\PassMiss\) pairs:
the affected operation set obtained on the operation-precedence graph is empty (\(T_{\mathrm{impact}}=0\)), the seed set and the reservation impact set are nonempty,
and the original schedule is infeasible after the blockage.
The first condition follows directly from the convention of Definition~\ref{def:task_impact} for Class B; the substantive one is the third, namely that the original schedule \emph{is} indeed infeasible.
Hence, in these pairs, the inference ``the operation-precedence graph yields an empty scope, so no rescheduling is needed'' never holds.

