\subsection{E2: containment}
\label{sec:e2}

This section tests whether two properties of the reservation impact set hold in the experiments.
\textbf{E2a, structural closure} (Proposition~\ref{prop:struct}): no dependency edge leads from the reservation impact set to an unfinished occupancy outside it.
This is a property of the graph, guaranteed directly by the definition; it checks that the implementation agrees with the definition.
\textbf{E2b, invariance outside the set} (Proposition~\ref{prop:outside}): after single-pass rerouting inside the impact set, every occupancy outside the set keeps its corridor, interval, and vehicle.
This is a property after repair; it checks whether the premises of that proposition are met in the experiments.
One premise requires that, before the repair starts, every occupancy outside the set can be written back into the new reservation table with its original fields (called \emph{pre-commit} below).
If an occupancy outside the set overlaps the blockage window, the pre-commit fails, which means that the seed set has missed an occupancy overlapping the blockage.

The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:e2}. In all \(\NPairs\) pairs, the impact set is closed;
single-pass rerouting inside the impact set (without scope escalation) restores feasibility; and every occupancy outside the set keeps its original values, i.e., no pre-commit failure occurs.
Because transport legs spanning \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) lie inside the impact set, E2b does not compare their already traversed segments;
we therefore separately check every occupancy with \(t^e\le t_{\mathrm{now}}\) one by one, and none is rewritten in any of the \(\NPairs\) pairs.

