\subsection{E3: boundary ablation}
\label{sec:e3}

This section tests whether, with the same rerouting procedure, replacing only the scope object changes the feasibility rate.
R1 and R2 use the same initial solution, the same decision time, the same random seeds, and the same blockage window, and both perform only single-pass rerouting;
\textbf{the only difference is which occupancies are rewritable}. Scope escalation is disabled in this section: if it were enabled, R1 could restore feasibility after escalating to all unfinished occupancies,
and the measurement would no longer reflect the difference between scope objects. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:e3} and Figure~\ref{fig:e1e3} (right).

Table~\ref{tab:e3} shows that in all \(\NPairs\) pairs, R1's rewritable set is empty whereas R2's is nonempty;
R1 is infeasible in every pair, and R2 restores feasibility in every pair. When R1's rewritable set is empty, single-pass rerouting changes no occupancy,
so the original schedule is kept as it is and still occupies the blocked corridor. R1 is therefore infeasible because it decides that no rescheduling is needed, not because its repair is poor.
The runtime of R2's single-pass rerouting has a median of \(\MsRepairMed\)\,ms and a maximum of \(\MsRepairMax\)\,ms.

Our corridor edges are equally weighted, i.e., every corridor has the same traversal time. To test whether the above results depend on this setting,
we rerun E1 and E3 on the same \(\NPairs\) pairs under three non-uniform edge weightings:
first, each corridor's traversal time is multiplied by an independent lognormal factor (geometric mean \(1\));
second, corridors farther from the load/unload station are slower, up to \(1.8\) times the original at the farthest;
third, each corridor randomly takes half or twice its original traversal time.
Under all three weightings, the three conditions of E1 still hold in every pair and R1 is still infeasible everywhere;
R2 restores feasibility in all pairs under the first two weightings and fails in \(1\) pair under the third (\(\WeightSplitRtwo\)).
Hence, neither the empty scope given by the operation-precedence graph nor R1's failure to restore feasibility changes with the edge weights.

The results of this section support contributions C2 and C3: replacing only the scope object changes the feasibility rate from \(\FeasRone\) to \(\FeasRtwo\).

