\subsection{E4: can network-structure indicators predict the impact-set size? (exploratory)}
\label{sec:e4}

This section tests whether indicators computed from the network alone, independent of the current schedule, can predict the size of the reservation impact set. We consider three indicators:
first, the \emph{LU cut}, i.e., the minimum number of corridors that must be cut to disconnect the load/unload station from all machines (the load/unload minimum cut);
second, the \emph{funnel share}, i.e., among the shortest paths from the load/unload station to the machines, the travel time on corridors that every path must use, as a share of the mean path travel time;
third, corridors per node, i.e., the ratio of corridors to nodes. We call these three indicators \emph{static network indicators} below.
The disturbance in this section blocks one corridor in the LU cut; all other settings are as in Section~\ref{sec:protocol}.
The two batches together contain \(8\) layouts, each with \(\NSeeds\) random seeds. The results are shown in Table~\ref{tab:e4pub}.

The last three rows of Table~\ref{tab:e4pub} show that the impact-set shares of the three spine layouts we constructed lie between \SelfCloLo\ and \SelfCloHi, a range of only \SelfCloSpread;
their geometric differences are also small (\(9\)--\(11\) nodes, \(8\)--\(12\) corridors, and an LU cut of only \(1\) or \(2\)).
Both ``the indicators are too coarse to reflect layout differences'' and ``these layouts differ too little'' are consistent with this,
\textbf{so our three layouts alone cannot tell whether the static network indicators are informative}, and the second batch is needed.

The five layouts of the second batch lie on only two grids (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}), so they can be used only to examine the machine-configuration dimension.
On the same \(4\times 4\) grid, changing only the machines from \(4\) to \(5\) (layout 2 to layout 3) lowers the median impact-set share from \PubCloHi\ to \PubCloLo, a difference of \PubCloSpread;
on the same \(5\times 5\) grid, three machine configurations (layouts 4--6) differ by \PubCloSpreadBig.
Our three layouts, whose networks all differ, differ from one another by only \SelfCloSpread; the former two differences are about four and one and a half times as large.
The comparison is not symmetric: the layouts of Lyu et al.\ change both the number and the positions of machines, whereas our three layouts change only the network and differ little in geometry,
so \SelfCloSpread\ is only a lower bound on the variation caused by changing the network, and the comparison does not show that machine configuration matters more than network geometry.
\textbf{Hence, with the network fixed, changing only the machine configuration already changes the impact-set share markedly; the machine-configuration dimension cannot be ignored.}

In the table, the LU cut is \(2\) and the funnel share is \(0\) on all five external instances.
The reason is that both grids place the load/unload station at a grid corner: with only up, down, left, and right moves, a corner has only two corridors, so the LU cut is fixed at \(2\);
and the shortest paths from the station to the machines share no corridor, so the funnel share is \(0\).
The Liu 2023 layouts in the van Os toolkit (not included in this batch because they allow diagonal moves) place the station at the midpoint of a grid edge, with out-degree \(3\).
The LU cut on practical layouts thus takes only a few values determined by the station position and does not vary continuously with network geometry.
The impact-set share varies by \PubCloSpread\ on these instances while these two indicators do not change, so they cannot explain this variation.
The third indicator (corridors per node) takes only two values over the whole batch (\(23/16\) and \(38/25\)) and likewise cannot explain the variation within one grid.
\textbf{Hence the static network indicators are constant on the layouts of Lyu et al.\ and cannot explain the variation in impact-set size.}
These indicators were designed for the adjustable load/unload exit bottleneck of spine layouts and degenerate to constants on practical layouts.

Conclusion: on the \NPubInst\ instances of the second batch and the three layouts we constructed, the static network indicators do not explain the impact-set size;
the impact-set size more likely depends on the spatial distribution of transport demand over the network than on how many routes the network can offer,
and specific candidate quantities are given in Section~\ref{sec:future}, ``Future work''.

