The results on the two instances constructed in this paper are shown in Table~\ref{tab:e5} and Figure~\ref{fig:e5}. Four points can be observed.

First, R0+ has a lower \(C_{\max}\) than R2 at every budget point, and the two curves in Figure~\ref{fig:e5} (left) do not intersect;
together with the external-layout batch, the R0+ win/tie/loss over the \(\EfivePts\) budget points is \(\EfiveWLT\).

Second, there is no crossover within the tested range, and this extends to larger budgets. The \(C_{\max}\) of R2 does not change with the budget;
R0+ reports the best-so-far value per generation, and with the random seed fixed, a larger budget only lets the same search trajectory run for more generations, so its \(C_{\max}\) is monotonically non-increasing.
Hence, once the order of the two is settled at the smallest budget level, increasing the budget cannot reverse it. At the smallest level, among the \(\EfiveCells\) pairs,
R0+ is strictly better in \(\EfiveWinMin\), ties in \(\EfiveTieMin\), and is worse in none; both the monotonicity and the budget invariance of R2
were checked pair by pair on both batches. Two statements should be kept apart: ``increasing the budget cannot reverse the order'' is guaranteed by monotonicity and holds for any instance;
``R0+ is no worse than R2 at the smallest budget'' is only an observation on these \(\EfiveCells\) pairs and need not hold on other instances.
The \(\EfivePts\) budget points come from \(\EfiveInst\) instances and \(\EfiveCells\) pairs, and the three budget points of a pair belong to the same search trajectory,
so they are not independent; this subsection therefore performs no significance test.

Third, this extension does not apply to lower budgets. R0+ needs at least one decoding per candidate evaluated, and one decoding takes about
\(\MsRedecode\)\,ms (median); with a population of \(40\) individuals, evaluating the initial population alone takes about \(0.17\)\,s,
close to the smallest tested budget of \(0.2\)\,s. Below \(0.2\)\,s, R0+ may therefore be unable to evaluate even one generation, and the order may reverse,
whereas R2 needs only milliseconds and is feasible in all pairs; this range was not tested.

Fourth, bounded repair yields a worse makespan at every budget point, but its runtime is two to three orders of magnitude lower
(\(\MsRepairMed\)\,ms for single-pass rerouting versus budgets of \(0.2\)--\(2\)\,s; see Figure~\ref{fig:e5}, left).
This runtime gap comes from a single rerouting pass that does not restart the search; it does \emph{not} come from scoping on the impact set,
as Section~\ref{sec:cheap} (comparisons within the millisecond range) confirms with the RA baseline of equal runtime.
The occupancy rewrite fractions of both methods are about \(0.6\) (last column of Table~\ref{tab:e5} and Figure~\ref{fig:e5}, right).

In the makespan comparison above, neither method rewrites finished occupancies. R0+ uses the fixed-prefix decoding of Section~\ref{sec:vs_r0};
if at \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\) a vehicle has already set off empty to pick up a job, while the preceding operation of that job is not yet finished and the pickup time must be redetermined,
R0+ keeps the original vehicle, leaves the stretch already traveled unchanged, and replans only the part after \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\).
A point-by-point check shows that R0+ rewrites finished occupancies \(\RzeroPastLo\) times at each of the \(9\) budget points of both instances,
and bounded repair likewise rewrites none in the same pairs. The makespan gap between the two therefore contains no contribution from ``rewriting finished occupancies'';
it comes from the advantage of global reassignment and resequencing over a single rerouting pass.

Conclusion: when a millisecond-scale response is required, bounded repair restores feasibility; when a response time of seconds is acceptable,
warm-started global rescheduling that does not rewrite finished occupancies achieves a better makespan. The value of the reservation impact set does not lie in the makespan;
its intended use is recovery that must respond within milliseconds.

