Because the two methods tie exactly in \(18\) of the \(50\) pairs, the lower end of the confidence interval for the \emph{median} paired difference is \(0\).

Excluding the \(6\) pairs with identical results leaves \(44\) pairs, with a pair-by-pair result of \RAStabWTLNet\ and a mean reduction of \RAStabMeanNet\
(under both counts, the Wilcoxon test has \(32\) pairs with nonzero differences); the reduction reported over all \NPairs\ pairs is thus conservative.
The direction of the reduction is highly consistent, but its \emph{magnitude is small}; in the only pair where R2 is worse, R2 rewrote \(0.007\) more.
Meanwhile, the two methods have nearly identical runtimes (\MsCheapRtwo\ vs.\ \MsAllFuture\,ms)
and nearly identical makespans (mean ratio \RACmaxRatio; pair-by-pair R2 win/tie/loss of \WinVsAllFuture, never worse,
with \RACmaxLo\ in the best pair).
