\subsection{Reproduction on the external-layout batch}
\label{sec:pubrepro}

This subsection tests whether the conclusions of E1, E2, E3, and E5 depend on the layouts we constructed. Setup: we use layouts 2--6 published by Lyu et al.\
(the second batch of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}), with \(\NSeeds\) random seeds per layout, \(\NPubPairs\) pairs in total,
and otherwise the same settings as in E1--E3 and E5; E5 runs only on layouts 2 and 6, with random seeds \(\{42,7,2024\}\)
and budgets \(\{0.2,1,2\}\)\,s, giving \(18\) budget points.

The results correspond item by item to those of the main batch.
E1: the operation-precedence graph yields an empty scope in \PubPassMiss\ pairs, while the original schedule is infeasible.
E2: the impact set is closed under dependency edges (\PubPassClosed), and occupancies outside the set are unchanged entry by entry (\PubPassInvar).
E3: R1 is feasible in \PubFeasRone\ and R2 in \PubFeasRtwo.
Impact-set share: computed as in E1, the per-layout medians of \(\mathrm{Cl}/|R|\) lie in \PubEoneFracLo--\PubEoneFracHi,
against a pooled median of \EoneFracMed\ over the \NPairs\ pairs of the main batch; the external batch is slightly lower, and both are about one half.
E5: at \(17\) of the \(18\) budget points R0+ attains the better \(C_{\max}\), at \(1\) point the two tie (layout 6, random seed \(2024\), budget \(0.2\)\,s),
and at no point is R0+ worse; these points are included in the pooled analysis of Section~\ref{sec:e5}, and there is still no crossover.

Conclusion: with the published layouts, the conclusions of E1, E2, E3, and E5 are unchanged, so the object-level conclusions of C1 and C2 do not depend on the layouts we constructed.

\subsection{Summary}
\label{sec:exp_summary}

We group the results of this section by the contribution level they support; the limitations and the levels they affect are collected in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}.

\textbf{The object level} (C1, C2: on which object the scope is delineated, and whether the resulting set is uniquely determined) is supported by four groups of results.
Under corridor blockage, the scope on the operation-precedence graph is empty although the original schedule is indeed infeasible (E1, \(\PassMiss\) over \(\NPairs\) pairs).
The reservation impact set is closed under dependency edges, and fields outside the set are unchanged entry by entry (E2, \(\PassClosed\), \(\PassInvar\)).
With the same rerouting procedure and only the scope object changed, the feasibility rate goes from \(\FeasRone\) to \(\FeasRtwo\) (E3).
Across four disturbance types crossed with two scope objects, the A/B dichotomy holds without exception,
and for Class A the reservation impact set always contains the scope obtained on the operation-precedence graph (E6).
These results correspond to the structural statements of Propositions~\ref{prop:miss}--\ref{prop:outside}.
Hence whether feasibility can be restored depends on the scope object, not on the rerouting procedure.

\textbf{The net-benefit level} (C3: what this way of delineating the scope gains). In E7, even the fastest global recomputation RD is slower than bounded repair
(\MsRedecode\ vs.\ \MsCheapRtwo\,ms), so the millisecond-scale response is not because the baselines ran too long but comes from single-pass rerouting.
In E5, warm-started global rescheduling is no worse than bounded repair on \(C_{\max}\) at every budget point, and relaxing the budget produces no crossover.
Switching to RA, which does not restrict the scope to the impact set (it uses the same rerouting as R2 and likewise does not rewrite finished occupancies),
leaves runtime and \(C_{\max}\) almost unchanged, yet raises the rewrite fraction from \ResFracCheapRtwo\ to \ResFracAllFuture\
(per pair \RAStabWTL, \(p=\RAStabP\); Section~\ref{sec:cheap}).
Hence the net benefit of scoping on the impact set lies in less rewriting, not in speed or makespan, and \textbf{this can be established only by comparing R2 with RA}.

\textbf{The remaining results} delimit where these conclusions apply: static indicators computed from the network alone cannot predict the impact-set size (E4);
when the share of disturbed operations reaches \(1\), bounded repair is still feasible in every pair and runs on the order of ten milliseconds (Section~\ref{sec:scale});
when the instance grows to \(\ScaleJobsHi\) jobs and \(\ScaleMachHi\) machines, the impact-set share relative to the unfinished occupancies does not rise (E8);
and with the layouts published by Lyu et al., the conclusions of E1, E2, E3, and E5 are unchanged (Section~\ref{sec:pubrepro}),
so the object-level conclusions do not depend on the layouts we constructed.

%% =====================================================================
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
%% =====================================================================

This paper makes three contributions. \textbf{First} (C1), it identifies a class of failures that the operation-precedence graph cannot capture: corridor blockage changes no operation assignment,
so the scope delineated by affected operations is empty, although the original schedule is indeed infeasible (all \(\NPairs\) pairs in E1, Section~\ref{sec:e1}).
\textbf{Second} (C2), it takes the reservation impact set obtained by \STRC as the rescheduling scope: once the dependency relations are given, whether an occupancy belongs to the set is
uniquely determined, and no dependency edge points from inside the set to outside it (satisfied in all \(\NPairs\) pairs in E2, Section~\ref{sec:e2}).
\textbf{Third} (C3), it provides a local recovery matched to this scope and separates the sources of the individual results: with the same rerouting procedure and only the scope object changed,
the feasibility rate goes from \(\FeasRone\) to \(\FeasRtwo\) (Section~\ref{sec:e3}),
so feasibility depends on the scope object rather than on the quality of the rerouting procedure.

Yielding relations, rerouting inside the set while keeping everything outside it as planned, and scope escalation after failure are all taken from the existing literature
(Section~\ref{sec:rw_claim}). Our claim is as follows.
By definition, the operation-precedence graph contains no yielding relations between corridor occupancies, and therefore cannot capture disturbances that invalidate only occupancies.
Once the scope is delineated on the reservation dependency graph, the occupancies that must be rewritten are uniquely determined by the given precedence relations, not by a heuristic radius.

If the rewritable set is changed from the reservation impact set to ``all occupancies unfinished after \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\)''
with all else unchanged, runtime and makespan remain almost the same, yet the rewrite fraction rises from \ResFracCheapRtwo\ to
\ResFracAllFuture\ (Section~\ref{sec:cheap}).
Hence the millisecond-scale response comes from single-pass rerouting without restarting the search; \textbf{the reduction in rewriting can be attributed only through this comparison,
and it reflects the property guaranteed by Proposition~\ref{prop:outside}: occupancies outside the set are kept as planned}.
The size of this benefit depends on how much smaller the impact set is than this trivial upper bound, and the benefit vanishes as the two approach each other.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

The following four limitations are ordered by their impact on the preceding results; Section~\ref{sec:future} gives a direction for addressing each of them.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=0.35em]
\item \textbf{Makespan.} Bounded repair trades makespan for runtime: over the \(\EfiveCells\) instance--seed pairs tested,
warm-started global rescheduling is no worse than bounded repair on \(C_{\max}\), and relaxing the budget produces no crossover (Section~\ref{sec:e5}).
This limitation concerns the net benefit of the scoping, not the object-level conclusions.

\item \textbf{Guarantee and size of the impact set.} Proposition~\ref{prop:outside} gives only a sufficient condition for occupancies outside the set to remain unchanged,
and the completeness of the dependency edge set has not been verified independently and exhaustively. Nor is the impact set the minimal set needed to restore feasibility:
it covers about \(\ClosureAliveFrac\) of the unfinished occupancies, fewer than one tenth less than including all of them in the rewrite,
so the reduction in rewriting from scoping on the impact set is limited, and the closer the impact set is to all unfinished occupancies, the smaller this benefit.
Moreover, static indicators computed from the network alone cannot predict the impact-set size (Section~\ref{sec:e4}).
This limitation concerns the strength of the object-level guarantee and the size of the impact set; it does not affect the conclusion of C1.

\item \textbf{Problem scope covered by the implementation.} Class B disturbances are handled by rerouting only; Class A additionally allows vehicle swapping and reassignment, but no change of processing order;
and, by Assumption A3, each disturbance receives a single response. Opening up the processing order degenerates into global rescheduling, at which point ``bounded'' loses its meaning;
several disturbances arriving one after another, estimation errors in disturbance information, and repeated rescheduling as new information arrives are not addressed.
The instance size has been extended to \(\ScaleJobsHi\) jobs and \(\ScaleMachHi\) machines without the impact-set share rising,
but E8 has only seven levels, which is insufficient to infer the trend at larger sizes. Extrapolation of the results to larger instances and to continual disturbances therefore remains limited.

\item \textbf{External reference points.} This line of research currently has no public benchmark that can be substituted in.
The public instance families~\cite{homayouni2021production,fontes2023hybrid} publish transport times as machine-to-machine travel-time matrices rather than as networks:
we checked all \(11\) layouts of the two families one by one, and none can be reconstructed as an undirected corridor graph consistent with the published matrix,
so the occupancy table \(R\), the reservation dependency graph \(G_R\), the reservation impact set \(\mathrm{Cl}(\cdot)\), and Class B disturbances cannot be defined on them (Section~\ref{sec:protocol}).
The layouts of the second batch are transcribed from the appendix of Lyu et al.~\cite{lyu2019approach}, but the per-leg travel times and job data are still set by us,
so the batch cannot be used to reproduce the reference values of Lyu et al.\ or van Os~\cite{vanos2026exact}, and its coverage is smaller than that of the main batch.
Hence only the networks of our instances come from the published literature, and the external validity of the results is limited; the relative comparisons among methods within the same implementation are unaffected.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Future work}
\label{sec:future}

The following four directions correspond one to one to the four limitations of the previous section.

\textbf{1. Narrowing the makespan gap} (Limitation \(1\)).
In the main batch, prefix decoding does not rewrite finished occupancies (the number of violations over the \(18\) budget points of E5 is \(\RzeroPastLo\),
and that of RD on E7 is \RedecodeBad), so Limitation 1 concerns only \(C_{\max}\) itself.
Narrowing the gap would require limited reassignment or resequencing without rewriting finished occupancies, but this would make the method degenerate toward global rescheduling.

\textbf{2. Making the guarantee of the reservation impact set more complete and tighter, and characterizing its size} (Limitation \(2\)).
\emph{Completeness}: under the current yield edge set, the leakage found by the probe is \ProbeLeaks.
The next step is to probe with other disturbance forms (e.g., shortening occupancies, or delaying them by more than \(1\) time unit), rather than to keep adding dependency edges of the same kind.
\emph{Tightness} points to a minimal rewritable set: define minimality for a fixed repair capability
and quantify the gap between the impact set and it. This is a necessary step to advance ``bounded'' from a sufficient condition to a tight bound; the current impact set covers about
\(\ClosureAliveFrac\) of the unfinished occupancies, so the gap may be considerable. \emph{Size characterization}: there is currently only one post hoc rule (the rewritable share, Figure~\ref{fig:worth}), which varies with the schedule and cannot be computed before the disturbance;
static indicators computed from the network alone are constant on practical layouts, while changing only the machine configuration on the same network already changes the impact-set share (Section~\ref{sec:e4}).
A next step is to examine schedule-dependent quantities, such as the number of mutually overlapping occupancies on the disturbed corridor near \(t_{\mathrm{now}}\),
and to predict the impact-set size from a given layout together with the current schedule.

\textbf{3. Relaxing Assumption A3} (Limitation \(3\); A3 stipulates a single, fully observed disturbance).
The next step is to relax this assumption and examine whether the impact set grows cumulatively under continual disturbances.

\textbf{4. Travel times from an external source} (Limitation \(4\)). The layouts already come from the published literature, but the per-leg travel times are still set by us.
The next step requires an instance family that publishes both a network and per-leg travel times; the non-uniform edge-weight sweep in E3 only tests the robustness of the results to the edge-weight setting
and cannot replace externally published travel times.

